{
 "section": "03",
 "slug": "03-09-nan-madol",
 "heading": "## 03 — #9 NAN MADOL, MICRONESIA",
 "text": "Number nine. Off the coast of Pohnpei, in Micronesia, more than a hundred artificial islets sit in a shallow lagoon. Their walls are long, six-sided basalt columns, stacked crosswise like a log cabin and packed with coral. This was the seat of the Saudeleur rulers, and its monumental building is associated especially with about twelve hundred to fifteen hundred C.E. The basalt was quarried elsewhere on Pohnpei, where volcanic rock naturally cools into columns. Then it was moved, possibly by raft, and stacked, one heavy column at a time. Exactly how is uncertain. Rafts, ramps and levers have all been proposed, and no written record settles it. What's clear is the organisation behind it: rulers who could command labour for generations. Moving stone around one island is hard. Try moving it across a country, five thousand years ago.",
 "text_chars": 840,
 "previous_text": "For each site: what survives today, how it was most likely built, and what nobody knows yet. And number one holds some of the largest stones ever set into a building.",
 "next_text": "Number eight: Stonehenge, built in phases over more than a thousand years. Its stones didn't all travel the same distance.",
